==============================================================================
READ THE RESULT
==============================================================================

  Q1 — the abscissa.  Read part 2: the sigma at which the mean decay slope
  crosses zero.  It should be 1.0.  If it is not, the instrument is wrong and
  part 3 is unreadable.

  Q2 — is 1/2 special?  Part 3's verdict: smooth through 1/2
       max departure / residual sd = 2.61

    STRUCTURE at 1/2 -> the Prime Beat's measured optimum at sigma = 0.50
        would have a convergence-side explanation, which is not in the
        literature as far as this work knows.

    smooth through   -> the optimum comes from the functional equation's
        reflection axis, and convergence has nothing to do with it.  The
        Beat's sigma = 1/2 is then the Mellin normalisation showing up, and
        the fact that it is measurable from primes alone remains true and
        remains unexplained by anything here.

  Limits: only four t values, chosen off the zeros; the N ladder is short;
  the decay-exponent regression assumes power-law behaviour in N, which is
  the right form for a Dirichlet series but is imposed rather than derived.


  results written to results/O9_xvar_prime.json
